 <h2 class="faq-details-section-heading" id="faq">

 FAQ
 </h2>

 <div class="faq-details-list">

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 What is effective altruism?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Effective altruism is the project of finding the best ways to help others and putting them into practice. It combines careful research into which interventions have the greatest impact with a global community that applies those findings to its careers, donations and everyday decisions.
 </p>
 <p>

 It is not a single cause or a single philosophy. It is a way of thinking that asks: how can we do the most good possible with the limited resources we have?
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/introduction-to-effective-altruism" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Introduction to effective altruism</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 How does effective altruism compare different types of "good"?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Effective altruism focuses on reducing suffering and increasing well-being. Comparing benefits across different people, countries, species or generations is hard, but imperfect comparisons are still more useful than no comparison at all.
 </p>
 <p>

 When one approach produces ten or a hundred times more impact than another, that order of magnitude matters even if the exact numbers remain uncertain. Researchers have developed tools like WELLBYs and welfare ranges to make these comparisons more rigorous.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/the-meaning-of-making-a-difference" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The meaning of making a difference</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://rethinkpriorities.org/research-area/welfare-range-estimates/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Welfare ranges (Rethink Priorities)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 What if I don't think effective altruism's practical suggestions are effective?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Effective altruism is a work in progress, not a closed list. Specific recommendations (which organizations to support, which careers to pursue) are hypotheses based on the best available evidence, and they get updated when new information appears.
 </p>
 <p>

 If you think there is a more effective cause or intervention, that is exactly the kind of contribution the movement is looking for. Reasoned, well-grounded disagreement is welcome.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/articles/disagreeing-with-80000-hours/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Disagreeing with 80,000 Hours (80,000 Hours)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Are you saying what I'm doing right now isn't the most effective thing?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Probably not. But that is good news: it means a reasonable change can give you much more impact with a similar level of effort.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism is not about blame; it is about offering information. If you find that changing cause or organization can multiply your impact, that is an opportunity, not a reproach.
 </p>
 <p>

 Evidence suggests that the differences between the best and worst interventions are usually very large, not marginal. That is why it is worth checking every so often whether what you are doing is still at the top of your list.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/some-causes-are-more-effective-than-others" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Some causes are more effective than others</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Does effective altruism only recommend things that are proven to work?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. The movement values evidence, but it also recognizes that some of the most important problems are hard to measure directly.
 </p>
 <p>

 Reducing catastrophic risks, improving global governance or researching future pandemics does not produce measurable results in the short term. Even so, it can be very valuable because of the scale of the problem, its relative neglect, and the chance of disproportionate impact.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism combines two approaches: causes with rigorous evidence, like global health, and more speculative but potentially enormous causes, like existential risks.
 </p>
 <p>

 The question is not only "what can be measured?", but "what makes sense to support given everything we know?".
 </p>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 I already pay my taxes. Why should I do more?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Paying taxes funds public goods in your own country, but it leaves out many of the world's most severe problems. People living in extreme poverty in other countries do not vote in your elections or receive your public services.
 </p>
 <p>

 The resources dedicated to global problems are surprisingly small compared to their scale. A modest donation can go much further when it is directed to efficient, neglected causes.
 </p>
 <p>

 And if you live in a high-income country, your tax burden is probably much smaller than you think in terms of the global well-being you could fund with a little more.
 </p>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 What if I don't have enough money to donate?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Effective altruism is not only about donating money. It is about using whatever resources you have (time, talent, attention) to do the most good possible. For many young people, the choice of career has far more impact than how much they donate while studying.
 </p>
 <p>

 If you live in a high-income country, you are probably wealthier than you think in global terms. A calculator like "How rich am I" shows where you stand worldwide. But even on modest income, choosing a career well can multiply your impact over decades.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://howrichami.givingwhatwecan.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How rich am I? (Giving What We Can)</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/career-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">80,000 Hours career guide</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Isn't effective altruism obvious?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 In principle, yes: who wouldn't want to do the most good possible? But in practice, very few people and organizations act consistently with that idea.
 </p>
 <p>

 Most philanthropic money is distributed without comparing options, without measuring results, and without prioritizing the most urgent causes. Effective altruism turns a seemingly trivial idea into a concrete practice that produces results very different from ordinary philanthropy.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/why-triviality-is-beside-the-point" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why triviality is beside the point</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 What things are people in the community working on?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 The effective altruism community works on a wide variety of causes. Some of the most prominent are: global health and development, animal welfare, global catastrophic risks, AI safety, biosecurity, improving institutional decision-making, and building the community itself.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Some reference organizations
 </div>
