 <h2 class="faq-details-section-heading" id="objections">

 Objections to effective altruism
 </h2>

 <div class="faq-details-list">

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Is effective altruism only about making money and donating it to charity?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. "Earning to give" is just one of many strategies, and for most people it isn't the best option. The movement's more recent research suggests that working directly on important problems usually has more impact than funding others to do it.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism spans careers in research, public policy, science, nonprofits, communications and many other fields. Donating is a tool, not the goal.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/articles/earning-to-give/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Earning to give: should you do it? (80,000 Hours)</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/key-ideas/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Key ideas of 80,000 Hours</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Is effective altruism the same as utilitarianism?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. Although utilitarianism is compatible with effective altruism and many of its early figures are utilitarians, the movement does not require committing to any particular ethical theory.
 </p>
 <p>

 People with very different values (deontologists, virtue ethicists, religious thinkers, pluralists) can share the basic idea that it is better to help more people than fewer when both options cost the same.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/the-definition-of-effective-altruism" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The definition of effective altruism (William MacAskill)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Does effective altruism neglect systemic change?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. In fact, several of the movement's core priorities are explicitly systemic: regulating artificial intelligence, improving pandemic preparedness, strengthening global governance, reducing animal suffering in industrial food systems, and improving the quality of institutional decisions.
 </p>
 <p>

 The critique usually assumes that "systemic" means only radical political change, but structural change takes many forms. Effective altruism evaluates systemic interventions with the same criteria it uses for direct interventions: importance, neglectedness and tractability.
 </p>
 <p>

 What the movement does ask is that any proposal for systemic change, like any other, comes with a clear argument about why it would work and at what scale.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.openphilanthropy.org/research/cause-reports/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open Philanthropy cause reports</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://forum.effectivealtruism.org/topics/systemic-change" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Systemic change (EA Forum)</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/articles/effective-altruists-love-systemic-change/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Effective altruists love systemic change (80,000 Hours)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 If everyone followed effective altruism's advice, wouldn't there be problems?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 If everyone followed effective altruism's recommendations, the world would be radically better: extreme poverty would disappear quickly, catastrophic risks would be taken seriously, and a great deal of suffering would be prevented.
 </p>
 <p>

 This objection usually assumes an absurd scenario, for example, that everyone would become a doctor in a remote area. In reality, the movement adapts its recommendations to the current margin: what has the most impact depends on what others are doing. It is dynamic advice, not a universal recipe.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://80000hours.org/articles/the-case-for-not-everyone-becoming-a-doctor/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The case against everyone becoming a doctor (80,000 Hours)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Is effective altruism calculating and impersonal?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Calculating in order to help more people isn't the opposite of compassion: it is compassion taken seriously. If you care about people, you should care that your help actually benefits them.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism combines reason with care. Reason helps decide how, where, and whom to help; care is the motivation behind it. One without the other is incomplete.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/four-ideas-you-already-agree-with" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Four ideas you already agree with</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/cheerfully" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cheerfully (Julia Wise)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Is effective altruism too demanding?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 It doesn't have to be. The movement does not demand extreme sacrifices. The goal is to do more good with the resources you already have, not to become a martyr.
 </p>
 <p>

 Many people in the community donate 10% of their income, choose high-impact careers, or spend part of their free time on relevant projects. Each person decides how much commitment to take on.
 </p>
 <p>

 What the movement does ask is that that commitment is well directed. Taking care of yourself and sustaining close relationships is not in tension with effective altruism: it is what makes it possible in the long term.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/excited-altruism" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Excited altruism (Julia Wise)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Doesn't charity start at home?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 "Charity begins at home" is often used to justify ignoring more urgent problems elsewhere. But resources don't go equally far everywhere.
 </p>
 <p>

 For example: US$40,000 funds, in a wealthy country, part of the training of a guide dog for a blind person. That same US$40,000, directed to trachoma prevention in a low-income country, can cure blindness in hundreds of people.
 </p>
 <p>

 This doesn't mean helping family or neighbors is wrong. It means it is worth asking where your help makes the biggest difference.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/the-moral-imperative-toward-cost-effectiveness" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The moral imperative toward cost-effectiveness (Toby Ord)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Does charity and aid really work?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Yes, but not all of it works equally well. Some interventions have very strong evidence of working, such as bednets for malaria, vitamin A supplementation, or direct cash transfers, while others have little impact or are even counterproductive.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism does not defend charity indiscriminately: it defends investing in what the evidence supports. Organizations like GiveWell specialize in finding the most effective interventions.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.givewell.org/how-we-work/our-criteria" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GiveWell's criteria</a></li>
 <li><a href="https://www.effectivealtruism.org/articles/does-foreign-aid-really-work" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Does foreign aid really work?</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Does donating to charity just subsidize billionaires?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. The legitimate concern behind this critique is that private philanthropy can replace the role of the state or serve narrow interests. But most effective donations go directly to organizations helping people in poverty, not to elite foundations.
 </p>
 <p>

 GiveDirectly transfers around US$30 million per year directly to families in extreme poverty, with no intermediaries. Open Philanthropy has moved over US$8 billion to rigorously evaluated causes. Bill Gates has donated over US$41 billion, largely to global health.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism has also been a pioneer in separating money from its source: donating well does not require admiring the donor. What matters is where it goes and what results it produces.
 </p>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 How are the people you're trying to help involved in decision-making?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 It is an important question. The best interventions are often those designed in collaboration with affected communities, or those that give them direct decision-making power, like cash transfers.
 </p>
 <p>

 Effective altruism recognizes this tension and takes it seriously. Organizations like GiveDirectly represent an approach that fully delegates the decision to those receiving the help. Others incorporate local feedback, participatory evaluations and ongoing consultation with communities.
 </p>
 <p>

 There are areas where the movement still has work to do, especially in involving affected people in setting priorities at the global level, not only at the program level.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.givedirectly.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GiveDirectly</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Does effective altruism neglect effective interventions when impact can't be measured?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 No. The movement has funded and promoted for years causes where measurement is very difficult: AI safety research, biosecurity policy, prevention of catastrophic risks, improving global institutions.
 </p>
 <p>

 What it does ask for is explicit reasoning: if an intervention cannot be measured directly, what is the argument for why it is valuable? Measurement is not the only way to think rigorously about impact.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.openphilanthropy.org/research/hits-based-giving/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Hits-based giving (Open Philanthropy)</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 <details class="faq-details">
 <summary>

 Is the effective altruism community too homogeneous?
 </summary>
 <div class="faq-details-body">
 <p>

 Yes, in several ways. The community has historically been whiter, more Western, more male and more highly educated than the general population. It is a problem acknowledged by both its leaders and its critics.
 </p>
 <p>

 There are active efforts to diversify the community: outreach programs in low-income countries, fellowships for underrepresented people, local chapters outside the Anglosphere (such as Altruismo Eficaz Bogotá), and continuous review of hiring and inclusion practices. There is still much to do.
 </p>
 <div class="faq-details-readmore">
 <div class="faq-details-readmore-label">

 Read more
 </div>
 <ul>
 <li><a href="https://www.centreforeffectivealtruism.org/diversity-and-inclusion" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CEA on diversity and inclusion</a></li>
 </ul>
 </div>
 </div>
 </details>

 </div>

